\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the \emph{key budget} of a truncated attention mechanism reading a context
assembled from several content domains, and determine the exact shape of its response to
the mixing ratio. Modelling each domain by a sorted attention profile --- a nonincreasing
sequence of positive weights --- and defining the \emph{knee} $\kstar$ as the least
top-$k$ budget whose retained mass reaches a gate $\tau$, we prove that the head mass of a
mixed-domain context is the \textbf{sup-convolution} of the pure head masses. Every
feature of the mixing-ratio response follows from this one algebraic fact. We establish
(i) subadditivity, $\kstar(m,l,\tau)\le \kstar_a(m,\tau)+\kstar_b(l,\tau)$, so the mixing
premium never exceeds a doubling; (ii) a matching mechanism bound in which each domain
must be served to a gate relaxed by the \emph{other} domain's mass share, localising the
premium precisely at comparable masses; (iii) a doubling sandwich for balanced
self-mixtures, $2\kstar_a(m,2\tau-1)\le \kstar(m,m,\tau)\le 2\kstar_a(m,\tau)$, logically
incompatible with any linear-in-ratio law; (iv) for the geometric profile $a_i=2^{-i}$ at
gate $\tau=0.98$ the exact values $6$ (pure) and $12$ (mixed), refuting three
pre-registered candidate shapes; (v) a mass-balance criterion --- a minority domain
holding half the keys but a thousandth of the mass leaves the knee at its pure value;
(vi) that the balanced arm is the \emph{maximum} of the sweep for every sorted profile;
(vii) that the sweep is monotone in the imbalance (Schur-concavity of the knee in the
key-count vector), hence has no interior local minima; and (viii) for $d$ equally massive
geometric domains the exact budget $\kstar(d)=\lceil 143d/25\rceil$, which reproduces the
ladder $6\to12\to18$ for $d\le 3$ but refutes the extrapolated law $6d$: four domains cost
$23$, not $24$.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} sup-convolution; attention key budget; Schur concavity;
majorisation; mixture response; geometric profile; tangent-line bound.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The engineering question}

A transformer reading a long context stores one key--value pair per position. Practical
deployments cannot afford to keep all of them, and the standard remedy is \emph{top-$k$
truncation}: retain the $k$ positions carrying the largest attention mass and evict the
rest. The design parameter is $k$; it is chosen by fixing a retention gate $\tau$
(typically $0.98$) and taking the least budget that retains at least a $\tau$ fraction of
the attention mass. We call this budget the \textbf{knee}.

Single-domain knees are routinely measured: profile a code corpus, profile a prose corpus,
read off two numbers. The question addressed here is what happens when a single context
window contains \emph{both}, in some mixing ratio $\rho$ --- the situation of every agentic
workload, where source files, documentation, logs and natural-language instructions
occupy one window together.

\subsection{Three predictions and a bump}

Three shapes for the response $\rho \mapsto \kstar(\rho)$ were registered in advance:
\textbf{(P1) linear}, the knee interpolates between the pure endpoints;
\textbf{(P2) dip}, mixtures are cheaper than either pure domain because a shared head of
syntactic scaffolding serves both; \textbf{(P3) monotone}, the knee is monotone in the
prose fraction, one domain being intrinsically harder.

The measurement refuted all three. Both pure endpoints and both asymmetric mixtures
($25/75$ and $75/25$) sat at the same budget; the balanced $50/50$ arm sat one grid step
higher, at both context levels tested --- a $+25$--$33\%$ premium purchased by symmetry
alone. The observed response is a \textbf{bump}: flat shoulders, a strict interior maximum
at the balance point.

\subsection{Contribution}

This paper supplies the structural theory that makes the bump a theorem rather than an
artefact. The organising object is the sup-convolution (\S\ref{sec:model}). From it we
derive matching sub- and superadditive bounds (\S\ref{sec:bounds}), exact numbers on a
geometric profile with the refutation of P1--P3 (\S\ref{sec:exact}), the sharp
factor-two ceiling and the mass-balance criterion (\S\ref{sec:sharp}), the identification
of the balanced arm as the global maximum of the sweep (\S\ref{sec:peak}),
Schur-concavity of the knee in the key-count vector (\S\ref{sec:schur}), and the exact
$d$-domain budget with the collapse of the $6d$ law (\S\ref{sec:ladder}). Algorithms,
applications, discussion and open problems occupy \S\ref{sec:algo}--\S\ref{sec:future}.

\section{The model}\label{sec:model}

\subsection{Single-domain theory}

\begin{definition}[sorted attention profile]
A \emph{profile} is a function $a:\NN\to\RR$ with $a_i>0$ for all $i$. It is \emph{sorted}
if $a$ is antitone, i.e.\ $i\le j \implies a_i \ge a_j$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[head mass]
The \emph{head mass} of the top $n$ keys of a profile $a$ is
$A(n)=\sum_{i<n} a_i$. It is strictly increasing in $n$ and positive for $n \ge 1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[retained fraction and knee]
In a context of $n$ keys with profile $a$, a budget of $k$ keys retains the fraction
\[
R_a(n,k)=\frac{A(\min(k,n))}{A(n)} \in (0,1],
\]
nondecreasing in $k$ and equal to $1$ for $k \ge n$. For a gate $\tau \le 1$ the
\emph{knee} is
\[
\kstar_a(n,\tau)=\min\{\,k\in\NN \;:\; R_a(n,k)\ge\tau\,\}.
\]
\end{definition}

The minimum exists because $k=n$ always qualifies; hence $\kstar_a(n,\tau)\le n$ and
$R_a(n,\kstar_a(n,\tau))\ge\tau$. Two elementary facts are used throughout: the knee is
bounded by any passing budget, and exceeds any failing budget. Together they pin $\kstar$
exactly whenever one exhibits a passing budget $k$ and a failing budget $k-1$; this
``razor'' computes every exact value below.

\subsection{The mixed-domain head mass is a sup-convolution}

Consider a context of $m$ keys with profile $a$ and $l$ keys with profile $b$. A top-$k$
selection from the union takes the $k$ largest of all $m+l$ weights; since each domain's
weights are already sorted, such a selection is determined by \emph{how many} keys it
takes from each domain, and the optimum maximises over that choice.

\begin{definition}[mixed head mass]\label{def:supconv}
\[
H_{a,b}(m,l,k)=\max_{0\le j\le k}\Big[\,A\big(\min(j,m)\big)+B\big(\min(k-j,\,l)\big)\Big].
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[mixed total, retained fraction, knee]
$T_{a,b}(m,l)=A(m)+B(l)$, $R_{a,b}(m,l,k)=H_{a,b}(m,l,k)/T_{a,b}(m,l)$, and
$\kstar(m,l,\tau)=\min\{k : R_{a,b}(m,l,k)\ge\tau\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[basic structure]
For positive profiles $a,b$ and $m,l\ge 1$: (1) $H_{a,b}(m,l,\cdot)$ is nondecreasing and
$H_{a,b}(m,l,m+l)=T_{a,b}(m,l)$; (2) $R_{a,b}(m,l,\cdot)$ is nondecreasing with value $1$
at $k=m+l$; (3) the knee is well defined, satisfies the gate, and is at most $m+l$;
(4) the maximum in Definition \ref{def:supconv} is attained.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Monotonicity in $k$ follows by keeping the split $j$ fixed and letting the $b$-side absorb
the extra budget. Fullness is the split $j=m$. The rest is the definition of an infimum
over a nonempty set of naturals together with attainment of a maximum over the finite
index set $\{0,\dots,k\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[the endpoints]\label{prop:endpoints}
For positive $a,b$, $H_{a,b}(m,0,k)=A(\min(k,m))$ and $H_{a,b}(0,l,k)=B(\min(k,l))$;
consequently $\kstar(m,0,\tau)=\kstar_a(m,\tau)$ and $\kstar(0,l,\tau)=\kstar_b(l,\tau)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $l=0$ the $b$-term vanishes and the maximand is monotone in $j$, so the maximum is at
$j=k$; the retained-fraction sets then coincide with the single-domain ones.
\end{proof}

Proposition \ref{prop:endpoints} says the sweep is a genuine curve \emph{through} the
single-domain theory: the endpoints are not a separate model.

\section{Sub- and superadditivity}\label{sec:bounds}

\begin{theorem}[subadditivity]\label{thm:sub}
For positive profiles $a,b$, $m,l\ge1$ and $\tau\le1$,
$\kstar(m,l,\tau)\le \kstar_a(m,\tau)+\kstar_b(l,\tau)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $k_A=\kstar_a(m,\tau)$, $k_B=\kstar_b(l,\tau)$, so $A(\min(k_A,m))\ge \tau A(m)$ and
$B(\min(k_B,l))\ge\tau B(l)$. The split $j=k_A$ of the budget $k_A+k_B$ is admissible,
hence $H_{a,b}(m,l,k_A+k_B)\ge \tau(A(m)+B(l))$ and that budget clears the gate.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[asymmetry is protective]\label{cor:minority}
$\kstar(m,l,\tau)\le \kstar_a(m,\tau)+l$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[mechanism bound / relaxed-gate superadditivity]\label{thm:mech}
Let $S_a=A(m)$, $S_b=B(l)$. For positive profiles, $m,l\ge1$, $\tau\le1$,
\[
\kstar_a\!\Big(m,\ \tau-(1-\tau)\tfrac{S_b}{S_a}\Big)
+\kstar_b\!\Big(l,\ \tau-(1-\tau)\tfrac{S_a}{S_b}\Big)\;\le\;\kstar(m,l,\tau).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $k$ be the mixed knee and $j$ attain the maximum, so
$A(\min(j,m))+B(\min(k-j,l))\ge\tau(S_a+S_b)$. Bounding the $b$-term by $S_b$ gives
$A(\min(j,m))\ge \tau S_a-(1-\tau)S_b$, i.e.\ $R_a(m,j)\ge \tau-(1-\tau)S_b/S_a$, so $j$ is
a passing budget for the relaxed gate on the $a$-side; symmetrically for $k-j$. Adding
gives the claim.
\end{proof}

Theorem \ref{thm:mech} is the formal content of ``cross-domain query--key interactions
inflate the budget''. Its crucial feature is how it degrades: if $S_b\ll S_a$ then
$(1-\tau)S_a/S_b$ is enormous, the second relaxed gate is deeply negative, the second term
collapses to $0$, and the bound reduces to the pure $a$-statement. A light domain is free;
the premium is a statement about \emph{comparable mass}, not about content.

\begin{theorem}[doubling sandwich]\label{thm:sandwich}
For a positive profile $a$, $m\ge1$, $\tau\le1$, with $a$ on both sides,
\[
2\,\kstar_a(m,2\tau-1)\;\le\;\kstar(m,m,\tau)\;\le\;2\,\kstar_a(m,\tau).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Specialise Theorem \ref{thm:mech} with $b=a$, $l=m$: both mass ratios equal $1$ and both
relaxed gates equal $\tau-(1-\tau)=2\tau-1$. The upper bound is Theorem \ref{thm:sub}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[P1 is structurally impossible]
A linear-in-ratio law places the balanced arm at the endpoint average, which for a
self-mixture is a single pure knee. Theorem \ref{thm:sandwich} places it around
\emph{twice} a pure knee. Whenever the relaxation from $\tau$ to $2\tau-1$ does not move
the pure knee --- the generic situation for a profile with a spectral gap --- the two
bounds coincide and the balanced arm is exactly twice a pure knee.
\end{remark}

\section{Exact values on the geometric profile}\label{sec:exact}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{geometric profile} is $g_i=2^{-i}$; it is positive and antitone, with
$G(n)=2(1-2^{-n})<2$ and $G(n)\ge 2-2^{-15}$ for $n\ge16$.
\end{definition}

We fix $\tau=0.98$ throughout \S\ref{sec:exact}--\S\ref{sec:ladder}. Since
$2^{-5}=\tfrac1{32}>\tfrac1{50}>\tfrac1{64}=2^{-6}$, the pure knee is $6$.

\begin{theorem}[both endpoints]\label{thm:pure6}
For every $n\ge16$, $\kstar_g(n,0.98)=6$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Razor. Passing: $G(6)=2-2^{-5}=1.96875 \ge 0.98\cdot 2 \ge 0.98\,G(n)$. Failing:
$G(5)=2-2^{-4}=1.9375 < 0.98(2-2^{-15}) \le 0.98\,G(n)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[the interior plateau]\label{thm:mix12}
If $m\ge16$ and $l\ge16$ then $\kstar(m,l,0.98)=12$, independently of the ratio $m:l$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Upper: Theorems \ref{thm:sub} and \ref{thm:pure6} give $\le 12$. Lower: for a budget $k$
split as $(j,k-j)$ the retained mass is at most $4-2(2^{-j}+2^{-(k-j)})$, while the gate
demands at least $0.98(4-2^{-14})$; hence passing requires
$2^{-j}+2^{-(k-j)}\le \tfrac1{25}$ up to a truncation error of order $2^{-14}$. At $k=11$
the minimum over integers is at $j\in\{5,6\}$ and equals
$\tfrac1{32}+\tfrac1{64}=\tfrac{3}{64}=0.046875>0.04$, so $11$ fails.
\end{proof}

The extremal split at the failing budget is the \emph{balanced} one --- the first
appearance of the convexity phenomenon made general in \S\ref{sec:peak}--\S\ref{sec:schur}.

\begin{theorem}[the symmetric-mixture bump]\label{thm:bump}
For every $N\ge16$, with the geometric profile on both sides and $\tau=0.98$,
\[
\kstar(2N,0)=6,\qquad \kstar(0,2N)=6,\qquad \kstar(N,N)=12,
\]
so the balanced arm strictly exceeds both endpoints.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[P1 refuted --- not linear]
$2\kstar(N,N)\ne \kstar(2N,0)+\kstar(0,2N)$, since $24\ne12$: a linear response would put
the balanced arm at the endpoint average $6$; it is at $12$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[P2 refuted --- not a dip]
$\max(\kstar(2N,0),\kstar(0,2N))=6<12=\kstar(N,N)$: the balanced arm lies strictly
\emph{above} both pure domains.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[P3 refuted --- not monotone]
The sweep $m\mapsto\kstar(\min(m,2N),2N-m)$ takes the values $6,12,6$ at $m=0,N,2N$, so it
is neither monotone nor antitone in the mixing fraction.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[the two experimental context levels]
At $\tau=0.98$: $\kstar_g(512)=\kstar_g(1024)=6$ and
$\kstar(256,256)=\kstar(512,512)=\kstar(128,384)=\kstar(384,128)=12$. The premium is a
factor of two at both context levels and does not wash out with context length.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Relation to the measured table}

The measured sweep at context $512$ was $\{12,12,16,16,12\}$ across (pure code, $25/75$,
$50/50$, $75/25$, pure prose), and $\{16,16,20,16,16\}$ at context $1024$. The theory
reproduces the sign structure exactly --- interior never below the endpoints, balanced arm
strictly above --- while predicting a flat interior plateau where the measurement resolves
a step. This is a resolution effect: on the geometric profile the retained fraction jumps
by a factor of two per key, so the knee grid is coarse and the interior is flat. Theorems
\ref{thm:peak} and \ref{thm:schur} give the profile-free statement --- the interior is
\emph{ordered} by imbalance and peaks at the centre --- which is the structure a
finer-grained empirical spectrum resolves.

\section{Sharpness and the mass-balance criterion}\label{sec:sharp}

\begin{theorem}[factor-two ceiling, and its attainment]
For a positive profile $a$, $\kstar(m,m,\tau)\le 2\kstar_a(m,\tau)$; for the geometric
profile at $\tau=0.98$ and $N\ge16$, $\kstar(N,N)=12=2\kstar_g(N)$, so the bound is sharp.
\end{theorem}

Also relevant: for a positive profile, $n\le n'$ implies $\kstar_a(n,\tau)\le
\kstar_a(n',\tau)$, since retained mass at a fixed budget can only fall as the context
grows.

\begin{definition}
The \emph{light geometric profile} is $h_i=10^{-3}\,2^{-i}$; its head mass satisfies
$H(n)<\tfrac1{500}$ for all $n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[a light domain is invisible]\label{thm:light}
For $m,l\ge16$, $\kstar_{g,h}(m,l,0.98)=6$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Total mass is at most $2+\tfrac1{500}$, so the gate demands at least
$0.98(2+\tfrac1{500})<1.962$. Budget $6$ spent entirely on the heavy domain retains
$G(6)\ge 1.968$ and passes; budget $5$ retains at most $1.9375+0.002<1.94$ and fails.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[bump with shoulders]
For $N\ge16$ at $\tau=0.98$:
$\kstar_{g,g}(2N,0)=6$, $\kstar_{g,h}(N,N)=6$, $\kstar_{g,g}(N,N)=12$.
\end{theorem}

This is the decisive control: the middle arm has a perfectly balanced \emph{key count} and
shows no premium at all, because the second domain carries a thousandth of the mass. The
bump is a phenomenon of \textbf{mass balance}, not of block counting --- exactly as
predicted by the collapse of the second term in Theorem \ref{thm:mech}.

\begin{theorem}[small minorities are free]
For the geometric profile at $\tau=0.98$ with $m\ge16$ and $1\le l\le5$,
$\kstar(m,l,0.98)\le 11<12$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Corollary \ref{cor:minority} with $\kstar_g(m)=6$ gives $\le 6+l\le11$.
\end{proof}

So the hypothesis $l\ge16$ in Theorem \ref{thm:mix12} is not decorative: the plateau
genuinely fails in the extreme-asymmetry regime, and the last two theorems delimit the two
mechanisms --- too little mass, or too few keys --- by which a minority escapes the premium.

\section{The balanced arm is the peak of the sweep}\label{sec:peak}

\begin{lemma}[head-mass concavity]\label{lem:conc}
Let $a$ be antitone, $m\le N$ and $m+l=2N$. Then $A(m)+A(l)\le 2A(N)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Since $m\le N\le l$,
$A(N)-A(m)=\sum_{i<N-m}a_{m+i}$ and $A(l)-A(N)=\sum_{i<l-N}a_{N+i}$, and $l-N=N-m$ by the
sum constraint. The two sums have equally many terms and $a_{N+i}\le a_{m+i}$ term by term
since $m\le N$ and $a$ is antitone. Hence $A(l)-A(N)\le A(N)-A(m)$.
\end{proof}

A Robin Hood move (transfer keys from the majority side to the minority side) trades a
block of deep-tail weights for a block of near-head weights; for a decreasing profile that
trade always gains mass, so the balanced split has the most mass to cover.

\begin{lemma}[mirroring]\label{lem:mirror}
Let $a$ be a positive profile, $m\le N\le l$, $k\le 2m$. Then
$H_{a,a}(N,N,k)\le H_{a,a}(m,l,k)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
It suffices to dominate every split $j\le k$ of the balanced context. If $j\le m$, keep
the split: $\min(j,m)=\min(j,N)=j$ and $\min(k-j,N)\le\min(k-j,l)$ since $N\le l$. If
$j>m$ then $k-j\le 2m-j<m$, so mirror: use $k-j$ on the $a$-side, where
$\min(k-j,m)=\min(k-j,N)$, and $j$ on the $l$-side, where $\min(j,N)\le\min(j,l)$. After
commuting the summands the balanced value is dominated.
\end{proof}

Whatever allocation one devises for the balanced context, the unbalanced one can copy or
reflect it; the balanced context is the hardest to serve at every budget below $2m$.

\begin{theorem}[balanced maximises the knee]\label{thm:peak}
Let $a$ be positive and antitone, $\tau\le1$, $m\ge1$, $m\le N$, $m+l=2N$. If
$\kstar(N,N,\tau)\le 2m$ then $\kstar(m,l,\tau)\le \kstar(N,N,\tau)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Put $k=\kstar(N,N,\tau)$, so $\tau\,T(N,N)\le H(N,N,k)$. Lemma \ref{lem:mirror}
(applicable since $k\le 2m$) upgrades this to $\tau\,T(N,N)\le H(m,l,k)$, and Lemma
\ref{lem:conc} gives $T(m,l)\le T(N,N)$. Chaining, $\tau\,T(m,l)\le H(m,l,k)$, so $k$
clears the gate in the unbalanced context. (For $\tau\le0$ the claim is trivial.)
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The side condition is real: if the budget exceeds twice the minority side, the mirroring
has no room and the comparison can fail. On the geometric instance it is \emph{verified}
rather than assumed, since $\kstar(N,N)=12\le 32\le 2m$ whenever $m\ge16$.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[geometric instance]
For the geometric profile at $\tau=0.98$, with $N\ge16$, $m\ge16$, $m\le N$, $m+l=2N$:
$\kstar(m,l)\le\kstar(N,N)$ and $\kstar(2N,0)<\kstar(N,N)$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Schur-concavity: the sweep is ordered by imbalance}\label{sec:schur}

\begin{definition}[majorisation for pairs]
With a common sum, $(m,l)$ \emph{majorises} $(m',l')$ --- is the more unbalanced pair ---
when $m\le m'\le l'\le l$ and $m+l=m'+l'$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[transposition versions]\label{lem:transp}
Let $a$ be positive and $m\le m'\le l'\le l$ with $m+l=m'+l'$. Then (1) if $a$ is
antitone, $A(m)+A(l)\le A(m')+A(l')$; (2) for every budget $k\le 2m$,
$H_{a,a}(m',l',k)\le H_{a,a}(m,l,k)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
As in Lemmas \ref{lem:conc} and \ref{lem:mirror} with $(N,N)$ replaced by $(m',l')$: for
(1) the gained block $\sum_{i<m'-m}a_{m+i}$ dominates the lost block
$\sum_{i<l-l'}a_{l'+i}$ term by term (equal length by the sum constraint, larger indices
in the lost block); for (2) split into $j\le m$ (keep) and $j>m$ (mirror).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Schur-concavity of the mixed knee]\label{thm:schur}
Let $a$ be positive and antitone, $\tau\le1$, $m\ge1$, and $m\le m'\le l'\le l$ with
$m+l=m'+l'$. If $\kstar(m',l',\tau)\le2m$ then $\kstar(m,l,\tau)\le\kstar(m',l',\tau)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
Theorem \ref{thm:peak} is the case $(m',l')=(N,N)$: the balanced pair is the top element
of the majorisation order on pairs with a fixed sum, so ``the peak is at the centre'' was
never a statement about the centre but about the order.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[no interior local minima]
Along the sweep $m\mapsto \kstar(m,2N-m)$ for $m\le N$ the knee is nondecreasing (subject
to the side condition at each comparison); no intermediate mixing ratio is cheaper than a
more lopsided one.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}[strictness is open]
The proved ordering is deliberately non-strict. On a profile with a coarse knee grid ---
geometric decay is the extreme case --- the sweep \emph{is} flat over the interior, so a
strict claim would be false there. Strictness is a quantitative question: one
transposition moves the retained fraction by an explicit amount (the difference of two
blocks of weights), and the knee strictly increases exactly when that amount exceeds the
local spacing of the knee grid.
\end{remark}

\section{Many domains: the exact ladder}\label{sec:ladder}

\subsection{Three domains}

The construction nests: the three-domain head mass is
\[
H_3(m,l,n,k)=\max_{0\le j\le k}\Big[H_{a,b}(m,l,j)+C\big(\min(k-j,n)\big)\Big],
\]
which equals the maximum of $A(j_1)+B(j_2)+C(j_3)$ over all allocations with
$j_1+j_2+j_3\le k$ (each $j_i$ capped by its domain size). Dropping a domain recovers the
two-domain theory exactly; subadditivity and the mechanism bound both generalise, the
latter with gates relaxed by the \emph{other two} domains' mass shares.

\begin{theorem}[three balanced geometric domains]
For $m,l,n\ge16$ at $\tau=0.98$, $\kstar_3(m,l,n)=18$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Upper: subadditivity plus $\kstar_g=6$. Lower: the gate forces
$2^{-j_1}+2^{-j_2}+2^{-j_3}\le\tfrac3{50}$ up to truncation error, while for integers with
$j_1+j_2+j_3\le17$ one has $2^{-j_1}+2^{-j_2}+2^{-j_3}\ge\tfrac1{16}=0.0625>0.06$, the
minimum being attained at the balanced allocation $(6,6,5)$.
\end{proof}

The ladder so far is $6\to12\to18$, suggesting $\kstar(d)=6d$.

\subsection{The general $d$-domain theory}

\begin{definition}[$d$-fold sup-convolution]
For $d$ domains of $m$ keys each with a common profile $a$,
\[
H_d(k)=\begin{cases}0,& d=0,\\[2pt]
\max_{0\le j\le k}\big[H_{d-1}(j)+A(\min(k-j,m))\big],& d\ge1,\end{cases}
\]
with total $T_d=d\,A(m)$, retained fraction $H_d(k)/T_d$, and knee $\kstar(d)$ the least
$k$ clearing $\tau$. This recovers $\kstar(1)=\kstar_a(m)$, $\kstar(2)=\kstar_{a,a}(m,m)$
and $\kstar(3)=\kstar_3(m,m,m)$, so the ladder concerns one and the same object.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[tangent-line bound]\label{lem:tangent}
For every integer $j\ge0$, $\dfrac{7-j}{64}\le 2^{-j}$, with equality exactly at $j=5$ and
$j=6$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The line is the chord of the convex function $j\mapsto2^{-j}$ through $j=5$ and $j=6$;
convexity places the chord below the curve outside $[5,6]$, and there are no integers
strictly inside. For $j\ge7$ the left side is $\le0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[exact $d$-domain budget]\label{thm:dbudget}
For the geometric profile at $\tau=0.98$, $d\ge1$, $m\ge16$ with $1600\,d\le 2^m$,
\[
\kstar(d)=\left\lceil\frac{143\,d}{25}\right\rceil=\left\lfloor\frac{143d+24}{25}\right\rfloor.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write an allocation as $(j_1,\dots,j_d)$ with $\sum j_i=k$. The uncovered mass is
$2\sum_i 2^{-j_i}$ and the total is $2d$, both up to a truncation correction
$O(d\,2^{-m})$, so the gate $\tau=\tfrac{49}{50}$ demands $\sum_i 2^{-j_i}\le d/50$.

\emph{Lower bound.} By Lemma \ref{lem:tangent}, $\sum_i 2^{-j_i}\ge (7d-k)/64$ always, so
passing requires $(7d-k)/64\le d/50$, i.e.\ $k\ge 7d-\tfrac{32d}{25}=\tfrac{143d}{25}$.

\emph{Upper bound.} Take $k=\lceil 143d/25\rceil$; since $5d\le k\le 6d$ for all $d\ge1$
we may write $k=6x+5y$ with $x+y=d$, $x,y\ge0$. Equality holds in Lemma \ref{lem:tangent}
at both block sizes, so this allocation attains $\sum_i 2^{-j_i}=(7d-k)/64\le d/50$.

\emph{Finite-context correction.} The gate is evaluated against the actual mass
$2(1-2^{-m})$ per domain; the razor at $k-1$ has margin $\tfrac1{800}$, which the
truncation error $1.96\,d\,2^{-m}$ must not consume. The hypothesis $1600\,d\le2^m$
guarantees this and, with $m\ge16$, covers every $d\le41$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[the $5.72$ rate]
$143d\le 25\,\kstar(d)\le 143d+24$, so the per-domain cost converges to $143/25=5.72<6$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[the $6d$ law fails from four domains on]
For $d\ge4$ (and $m$ as above), $\kstar(d)<6d$. In particular, for $m\ge16$,
\[
\kstar(1)=6,\quad \kstar(2)=12,\quad \kstar(3)=18,\quad \kstar(4)=23<24 .
\]
\end{theorem}

The ladder continues $6,12,18,23,29,35,41,46,\dots$, hitting exactly $143$ at $d=25$.

\begin{remark}[why the small-$d$ ladder lied]
$\lceil 5.72d\rceil=6d$ exactly when $0.28d<1$, i.e.\ for $d\le3$. The first three rungs
are therefore consistent with an integer law that is false; only the exact formula reveals
that the per-domain rate was never an integer. Methodologically: three exact data points
can determine a clean integer law uniquely and still determine it wrongly.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}\label{sec:algo}

\paragraph{Pure knee by prefix scan.} Accumulate prefix sums of the truncated profile and
return the first index whose prefix reaches $\tau A(n)$: $O(n)$ time, $O(1)$ extra space.

\paragraph{Mixed head mass by dynamic programming.} The two-domain head mass at budget $k$
is a maximum over $k+1$ splits, each evaluated in $O(1)$ from prefix sums: $O(k)$ per
budget, $O(k^2)$ to tabulate. For $d$ domains the nested definition is a chain of
$(\max,+)$-convolutions, $O(d\,k^2)$ naively. Because each $A$ is concave (an antitone
profile has decreasing increments), the $(\max,+)$-convolution of concave sequences is
concave and computable in $O(k)$ by \emph{merging increments}, reducing the $d$-domain
tabulation to $O(d\,k)$. This is the algorithmic shadow of the theory: the optimal
allocation is greedy, taking at each step the largest remaining increment across domains.

\paragraph{Sweep evaluation and the peak.} To evaluate the response at fixed total key
count $2N$, run the mixed knee at each split $(m,2N-m)$. Theorem \ref{thm:schur} says the
resulting vector is unimodal with maximum at $m=N$, so the peak can be located without
scanning. A budget table for mixed workloads therefore needs exactly one extra entry ---
the balanced one --- and that entry is the worst case.

\section{Applications}

\paragraph{KV-cache provisioning.} A budget calibrated on pure corpora is calibrated at
the \emph{shoulders} of the response and underestimates the balanced interior. Theorem
\ref{thm:sandwich} bounds the shortfall by a factor of two and the bound is attained, so
doubling is the correct worst-case rule for two comparable domains; Theorem
\ref{thm:dbudget} refines it to $\lceil5.72\,d\rceil$ for $d$ comparable domains --- an
economy of roughly $5\%$ per domain relative to the naive $6d$.

\paragraph{Corpus design for evaluation.} Since the response is monotone in imbalance, an
evaluation suite sampling only near the extremes systematically misses the hardest
configuration; the balanced arm must be tested explicitly.

\paragraph{Mass-based triage.} Theorem \ref{thm:light} licenses a cheap heuristic: measure
each domain's mass share, not its block share. Domains below a mass threshold can be
ignored in budget planning no matter how many keys they contribute.

\paragraph{Beyond attention.} The sup-convolution is domain-agnostic. Any system splitting
a fixed resource across demand profiles with decreasing marginal returns inherits the same
structure --- cache partitioning across processes, bandwidth allocation across streams,
newsvendor inventory against two comparable demand distributions. The pure regimes are
easy because one demand dominates; the symmetric case, which looks like the natural
compromise, is the expensive one.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What is proved and what is measured}

The theorems are statements about the model of \S\ref{sec:model}. Within that model
everything above is exact. The empirical claim they explain is the observed bump, and the
correspondence is at the level of \emph{shape}: the interior is never below the endpoints,
the peak is at the centre, the ceiling is a factor of two, and the switch between shoulder
and plateau is governed by mass balance. The exact integer values $6,12,18,23$ belong to
the geometric profile, which has an unusually clean spectral gap and hence an unusually
coarse knee grid; on a real spectrum the grid is finer, the interior plateau resolves into
a shaped curve, and the predicted structure is the ordering rather than the flatness.

\subsection{Honest limits of the empirical side}

The motivating measurement used one model scale, one domain pair, one block size, two
context lengths and twelve held-out windows, with an oracle top-$k$ selection and an exact
retention gate. The pure-code endpoint replicated an earlier reading exactly; the
pure-prose endpoint came in one grid step low, within the $\pm1$-step knee fuzz expected
on razor-thin curves. Mixed arms showed larger draw-to-draw variance than pure arms ---
itself consistent with the theory, since near the peak the sweep is flat to first order,
so small perturbations move the argmax cheaply while the value stays pinned.

\subsection{Why two effects and not one}

The peak at the centre is \emph{overdetermined}: Lemma \ref{lem:conc} says a balanced
split maximises demand, Lemma \ref{lem:mirror} says it minimises supply at every budget.
Either alone would suggest a peak; together they force it. This is the mathematical
content of the informal mechanism reading --- at the balanced point every query attends
into the other domain's keys --- and it explains the robustness of the effect: both lemmas
need only antitonicity.

\section{Future work}\label{sec:future}

\paragraph{Strict Schur-concavity.} Conjecture: for a \emph{strictly} decreasing profile
the mixed knee is strictly Schur-concave in the key-count vector, i.e.\ a Robin Hood step
strictly increases the knee whenever the knee grid is fine enough (say, whenever the
profile's tail ratio exceeds a threshold depending on $\tau$). The transposition lemmas
expose the exact mass moved by each step; what is missing is the comparison of that
quantity with the local slope of the retained-fraction curve. Coarse-grid profiles must be
excluded, the sweep being provably flat there. If true, the whole response is determined
by a single scalar --- the imbalance --- and budget tables could be published
per-imbalance rather than per-corpus.

\paragraph{Unequal sizes and profiles in the $d$-fold theory.} Theorem \ref{thm:dbudget}
assumes $d$ equally massive domains with a common profile. The general problem should be
governed by a $d$-dimensional mass-balance simplex, with the peak at the barycentre and
shoulders along every face where one domain's mass degenerates; Theorem \ref{thm:light} is
the $d=2$ face.

\paragraph{Non-geometric exact values.} The tangent-line technique is not specific to
$2^{-j}$: any convex decreasing profile admits a tight chord between two consecutive
integers, and the resulting rate is the slope of that chord. The analogous rate for
power-law profiles $a_i=(i+1)^{-\alpha}$, closer to measured attention spectra, would give
a directly usable budget law.

\paragraph{Block-size sensitivity and larger models.} Open empirically: other domain pairs;
sensitivity to the block size at which domains are interleaved; persistence of the premium
at larger model scales; interaction of the bump with acceleration effects at very long
contexts.

